---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Motif from "../components/Motif.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="About Rev. Sabato Morais (1823–1897), Italian-born hazzan of Congregation Mikveh Israel and founder of the Jewish Theological Seminary, and the Sabato Morais Digital Repository."
>
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-16 sm:py-20">
    <header class="mb-10 text-center">
      <p class="eyebrow mb-3">Biography</p>
      <h1 class="font-heading text-4xl leading-tight text-navy sm:text-5xl">
        Rev. Sabato Morais
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-2 font-heading text-lg italic text-burgundy">
        <span lang="he" dir="rtl">שבתאי מוראיס</span> &middot; April 13, 1823 &ndash; November 11, 1897
      </p>
      <div class="ornament mx-auto mt-6 max-w-sm">
        <Motif class="text-gold" size={20} />
      </div>
    </header>

    <!-- Portrait -->
    <figure class="mx-auto mb-12 max-w-sm">
      <div class="frame-gold overflow-hidden rounded-sm bg-paper-dark p-1.5 shadow-md">
        <img
          src="/images/sabato-morais.jpg"
          alt="Rev. Sabato Morais, hazzan of Congregation Mikveh Israel, Philadelphia (1823–1897)"
          width="900"
          height="700"
          class="w-full rounded-[2px] object-cover [filter:saturate(0.96)_contrast(1.03)]"
          loading="eager"
        />
      </div>
    </figure>

    <div class="prose prose-neutral max-w-none font-body text-lg text-ink prose-headings:font-heading prose-headings:text-navy prose-a:text-burgundy prose-strong:text-ink prose-blockquote:border-gold prose-blockquote:font-body prose-blockquote:text-ink">
      <p>
        <strong>Sabato Morais</strong> was the Italian-born Sephardic religious
        leader (<em>hazzan</em>) of Philadelphia's Spanish and Portuguese{" "}
        <strong>Congregation Mikveh Israel</strong> from 1851 until his death in
        1897. Born and educated in Livorno, in the Italian duchy of Tuscany, on
        April 13, 1823, Morais brought with him to the United States a Sephardic
        Italian rabbinic humanist outlook that he promoted throughout his ministry
        as a basis for Jewish religious Americanization. He received rabbinical
        ordination in Livorno in 1846 and was awarded an honorary Doctor of Laws by
        the University of Pennsylvania in 1887.
      </p>

      <p>
        He is best known as the principal founder and first president of the
        original <strong>Jewish Theological Seminary</strong> (JTS) in New York City
        in 1886, established to train rabbis in the traditions of abnegation, duty,
        and service in which he was raised. The seminary was reorganized after his
        death and incorporated in 1902 under a new name — the Jewish Theological
        Seminary of America — and over time promoted a new ideology as a center of
        Conservative Judaism.
      </p>

      <h2 id="life">A Scholar and Public Voice</h2>

      <p>
        Morais was an enlightened observant Jewish teacher and preacher, writer,
        translator, and poet, who corresponded in English, Italian, Hebrew, and
        French. By many accounts the outstanding Hebraist in America in the
        nineteenth century, he translated the Book of Jeremiah for the Jewish
        Publication Society. He was both a devoutly religious and a worldly, cultured
        man, who loved music — particularly opera — and was a close reader of Dante,
        Petrarch, and Shakespeare.
      </p>

      <p>
        An ardent Republican in the mode of Mazzini and Lincoln, he was an outspoken
        intellectual whose public concerns — religious freedom, human rights,
        opposition to slavery and the death penalty — were national and
        international in scope. He lectured publicly on a regular basis and published
        voluminously in the Jewish and general press. He was the first strictly
        observant American Jewish leader to support women's right to vote on
        congregational matters.
      </p>

      <p>
        He worked among the immigrant poor and taught at the local Jewish orphanage,
        the Hebrew Sunday School, the Hebrew Education Society, and at Maimonides
        College — the first rabbinical seminary — in Philadelphia. He mediated
        volatile labor disputes and denominational fractures. He vehemently opposed
        political Zionism while passionately defending Jewish peoplehood and
        universal principles, and he promoted communal unity and ecumenical harmony
        while opposing religious reform and missionary activity.
      </p>

      <h2 id="legacy">Legacy</h2>

      <p>
        After his death, Cyrus Adler — the first operating president of Dropsie
        College and an influential American Jewish communal leader — memorialized
        Morais as
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        <p>
          the representative American Jew to his co-religionists in England,
          France, Italy, and the Orient.
        </p>
      </blockquote>

      <p>
        The <em>New York Times</em> remembered Morais as
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        <p>
          the most eminent rabbi in this country&hellip; a powerful and aggressive
          factor in discussions of vast import and interest to millions of people; a
          deep, incisive, fearless thinker, speaker, and writer.
        </p>
      </blockquote>

      <p>
        His funeral procession, accompanied by thousands of mourners, was the first
        mass funeral in American Jewish history.
      </p>

      <h2 id="repository">About the Repository</h2>

      <p>
        The <strong>Sabato Morais Digital Repository</strong> gathers digitized
        letters, sermons, and manuscripts by and about Morais and makes them openly
        available for reading and research. Each item pairs a scholarly{" "}
        <abbr title="Text Encoding Initiative">TEI</abbr> transcription with
        high-resolution page images delivered through{" "}
        <abbr title="International Image Interoperability Framework">IIIF</abbr>, so
        readers can study the text and the original document side by side, with full
        descriptive metadata below.
      </p>

      <p>
        This repository is a project of{" "}
        <a href="https://judaicadhpenn.org/">Judaica Digital Humanities at Penn</a>, and
        forms part of the <strong>Jesselson-Kaplan American Genizah Project</strong>.{" "}
        For more about Morais and our project to transcribe, translate, and make his
        writings and correspondence publicly available, see{" "}
        <a href="https://www.library.upenn.edu/kislak/judaicadh/blog/letterswithoutborders">Letters Without Borders</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
